CPU handback and ownership
==========================

Run from this worktree:

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_handback_test.sh

Optional userspace sanitizers:

  CC=clang CFLAGS='-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g' \
    bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/cpu_handback_test.sh

The script compiles the actual reclaim_cpu(), mk_do_cpu_add(), mapping and
CPU-set functions with mocked native CPUHP/device operations. Its nineteen
scenarios check caller ordering, not a real CPU transition or concurrency.

Contract
--------

- The resource controller lock covers handback and ownership updates.
- Validate the logical CPU before indexing per-CPU storage. Reserve local
  ownership before device registration or native bringup can run.
- Register an absent enumerated CPU under the device-hotplug lock. Presence
  restoration, bringup and failure rollback are serialized by the native
  device-hotplug/device/CPU-map locks. The CPU write lock is taken separately
  for mask changes; _cpu_up() manages its own write-lock section.
- OFFLINE and DEPARTED are the supported starting CPUHP states. Native hotplug
  disable, SMT policy, possible-CPU and NUMA checks still apply. Successful
  return requires the CPU to be online at CPUHP_ONLINE, with device bookkeeping.
- Known preflight rejection undoes only newly added local ownership; it does
  not remove free-pool membership. The attempted output reports entry into
  native bringup, not whether firmware definitely started the hart/CPU.
- Preserve pool membership during the wake callback, which selects the x86
  local park slot. Never hold the pool lock across native bringup. After an
  attempted failure, retain local ownership and remove free-pool eligibility
  before releasing the resource controller lock, even for -EBUSY.
- Restore the original presence mask after a failed absent-CPU bringup only
  if the CPU is offline. An incomplete rollback must not make an online CPU
  absent. No automatic retry follows a failed attempt; CPUHP rollback and
  an errno are not proof that physical execution has stopped.
- Ordinary add_cpu() remains unchanged. The CPU-device NUMA bookkeeping is
  shared with the ordinary online callback and runs outside the CPU-map lock.

Coverage and limits
-------------------

The fixture checks successful and first-local bringup; allocation and device
registration failure; native hotplug/SMT/NUMA rejection; invalid CPUHP state;
attempted failure, attempted -EBUSY, incomplete rollback and false success;
impossible/out-of-range mappings; successful registration; preexisting
ownership; originally present failure; and already-online idempotence.

The native _cpu_up() implementation, scheduler, NUMA sysfs link changes and
firmware are not executed by this fixture. Mock lock assertions do not replace
live lockdep. Final x86 and RISC-V stack builds and native lifecycle tests are
separate gates. Mailbox quarantine and park-memory lifetime protection must
also land before claiming safe recovery from an uncertain x86 wake attempt.
